# karr-foundation: coordinated execution across boards

Status: design agreed 2026-08-17. Instance-specific parts live outside karr.

## What this is for

Today `karr-foundation` drains one board at a time: it runs an agent command in
a repo until the board stops moving, then goes to the next repo. That is enough
for independent repositories. It is not enough for a fleet whose repositories
depend on each other, where the order of work matters, where several different
agent commands are available with different strengths and different failure
modes, and where a human wants to be asked rather than surprised.

This is the generic half of that. Everything domain-specific — what "done" means
for a distribution, how a release is verified, which project depends on which —
stays out of karr and reaches it through a hook.

## The shape

Three layers. karr owns the first two.

**Coordination (shared).** Tickets, dependencies, escalations, the chain of
planned steps, open questions, the run log. All of it lives in git refs and
syncs with the remote, so every machine and every person sees the same picture.

**Execution (local).** Which agent commands exist on this machine, whether they
currently work, when to try them again, how many may run at once. Never in the
repository — an agent command that exists on one machine may not exist on
another, and an account limit is a property of a person, not of a project.

**Judgement (an agent).** Planning, routing, reacting to the unexpected. This is
a coordination agent, invoked like any other agent, and only when a written plan
is missing or has broken.

## The chain

An AI plans, written steps execute, the AI is called again only on deviation.
The AI is the compiler, the chain is the program, karr-foundation is the VM.
Between deviations no AI runs at all, which is what makes the whole thing
affordable.

A step names no agent. Naming one would tie the chain to a machine, and the
chain is shared state.

    id: 42
    kind: ticket            # ticket | shell | question | plan
    repo: /path/to/repo
    ticket: 41
    needs: [40, 41]         # step ids
    timeout: 1800
    precheck: ticket_status == todo
    on_stall: plan
    on_rate_limit: requeue

The chain is a DAG. Steps without a `needs` edge between them may run
concurrently; the planner does not serialise by hand, it simply leaves edges
out.

A step whose `precheck` no longer holds is not executed. It is marked stale and
calls the planner. This is what keeps a chain that has gone out of date from
doing damage — it costs time, not correctness. For the same reason chains stay
short: a long chain goes stale faster than it is worked through.

Chains are stored under `refs/karr-foundation/chain/*`, questions under
`refs/karr-foundation/questions/*`, run logs under
`refs/karr-foundation/log/<date>-<id>`. These are outside the protected
`refs/karr/*` namespace, so the existing helper-ref machinery applies — but
`karr sync` currently synchronises `refs/karr/*` only, and this namespace needs
the same treatment.

## One ticket per agent run

An agent invocation is exactly one ticket, never "drain this repo".

The point is control, not granularity. A single-ticket run ends by itself, so
nothing has to stop it, and the coordinator gets a decision point after every
ticket — which is where reordering, agent switching and gating happen. An agent
that dies mid-work loses one attempt: its claim expires, `karr unlock` clears
the pick lock, the ticket is free again.

## Agents and availability

karr knows the least it can about an agent. No cost, no tokens, no quotas — a
rate limit and an exhausted budget look identical from the outside (the command
stops working), so one mechanism covers both.

    minimax:
      command: claude_with_minimax
      kind: claude-code     # the contract: how arguments are appended, how the result is read
      probe_every: 15m      # optional; otherwise found by trying
      description: >-
        Prose. What this agent is good at, where it is weak, what it costs.
        This is the selection criterion. Not a class, not an enum — the
        thing choosing is a language model, and it reads better than it
        matches taxonomies.

Per-agent state: `ok` / `failing since X` / `next attempt at Y`. Where the reset
rhythm is known it is configured; where it is not, karr-foundation retries at a
fixed interval and **records when it worked again**. Reading a pattern out of
those records is the coordination agent's job, not a learning algorithm inside
karr.

`kind: claude-code` tells the coordination agent that it may append `-p`,
`--output-format json`, `--permission-mode`, `--allowed-tools`, and how to read
the result. Permission escalation therefore becomes a per-invocation decision
rather than a property baked into a wrapper script.

A run is classified from its result JSON, not by matching patterns against its
transcript. The current text scan exists because there was nothing better to
read; with a structured result there is.

## Routing without a taxonomy

The operator writes prose describing how they want their agents used — which
ones are cheap, which are trusted with delicate work, what to do when one is
unavailable, roughly how many of each may run at once. Those numbers are
estimates of where a session limit sits, not limits themselves; they are allowed
to be wrong, because being wrong marks the agent `failing` and the fallback
takes over.

The coordination agent reads that prose together with the agent list and the
current availability, and writes an assignment with fallback chains — repo path
to ordered agent list, with an explicit `WAIT` entry for "rather wait than use
something unsuitable here".

**No AI runs in the hot path.** karr-foundation looks the assignment up, takes
the first agent in the chain that currently works, and waits if none does. The
coordination agent is called only when the assignment has to be made again.

## Concurrency

Three levels, tightest wins: a hard machine ceiling (local, protects CPU and
memory), the operator's per-agent estimates from the prose, and the limits a
particular run declares in its chain header.

    limits:
      concurrent: 4
      per_agent:
        minimax: 2

One hard rule stays: **one agent per repository.** `.karr.lock` is per repo and
should remain so — two agents in one working tree collide. Concurrency is
therefore concurrency across repositories, never inside one. Changing that would
need a git worktree per agent, which is deliberately out of scope.

This means the runner has to become concurrent. It currently executes one
command after another, and without that change every concurrency setting is
decoration.

## Questions are objects

A question is a file with an answer field, not a dialogue. That removes the
special case for "a human happens to be present".

`questions/0007.yaml` carries the question, its context, the options, a default,
and a policy for what happens when nobody answers (`block`, `use_default`,
`escalate_to_ai`). The chain continues with everything that does not depend on
it. Who fills in the answer — a person at a terminal, a chat message, or the
coordination agent — is not something the chain needs to know.

## Escalation across repositories

The most common real-world interruption is "this cannot be done until X is fixed
somewhere else". An agent must neither give up nor reach into the neighbouring
repository. It reports:

1. `karr create` in the other repo, tagged `escalated-from:<repo>#<id>`
2. `karr edit <id> --block "needs <repo>#<id>: <reason>"` in its own
3. release the claim, exit

The coordinator sees both sides on the next cycle. Order is therefore not
planned, it is discovered — which is why work rotates across repositories rather
than finishing one before starting the next: a newly discovered prerequisite
simply moves forward in the rotation instead of breaking a plan.

`--depends-on` is board-local today. A tag convention plus a hub board carries
the link to begin with; cross-board dependencies make it explicit.

## The hub

One repository of a fleet carries the fleet-wide namespace. That is a role, not
a separate project: any repository can take it, and a fleet without a dedicated
coordination repository designates one of its own. Local configuration names it.

## The domain hook

When a board drains, `on_drained` runs a configured command. karr does not know
what it does. In the fleet this design came from it starts a release gate that
builds, installs, verifies against dependents and raises version requirements —
none of which karr should ever learn about.

